\section{Evaluation protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

\paragraph{Data.}
We use the public MTEB results~\citep{muennighoff2023mteb} at one pinned commit of the results repository (Section~\ref{sec:release}). A benchmark is one text task; a cell is a model's main score on it, in points on a 0--100 scale. We keep one revision per model and the modal dataset revision and language set per task, drop configuration variants and duplicate model names, and keep tasks with at least 50 models and models with at least 30 tasks, iterated to a fixed point. The result is a matrix of $M=238$ models and $N=328$ tasks with 45{,}775 observed cells (41\% missing) and 83 organisations (Appendix~\ref{app:data}, Table~\ref{tab:coverage}).

\paragraph{Held-out units and splits.}
The unit of hold-out is the organisation: models from one organisation are fitted together and held out together, since a lab's models share training data and recipes and a split by model would let a sibling stand in for the new model. The 83 organisations are assigned to 10 folds, and every model is held out once, with standardisation, factor model, rank, and margins fitted on the other folds only (the group split, G). Two further splits stress the setting: a temporal split trains on the 70\% earliest-released dated models and tests on the later ones (T; 68 test models from 37 organisations), and a strength split trains on the weakest 75\% by an additive model offset and tests on the strongest quarter (S; 60 test models from 32 organisations).

\paragraph{Pool and target set.}
Scoring a rule on the cells left unrun is confounded: different rules reveal different benchmarks, so the target changes with the rule and with $k$ (a rule that reveals the hardest benchmarks removes them from its own target and can score below the floor). We therefore split the 328 benchmarks at random into a pool $C$ of 219, from which a rule may choose, and a target set $T$ of 109 that no rule can choose, stratified by task type. For each held-out model the run set $S\subseteq C$ is chosen among the benchmarks the model has in $C$, and every rule and every inference method is scored on the model's observed cells in $T$. The floor is the same prediction with all of the model's observed cells in $C$ revealed. A pair (model, split) is eligible if the model has at least $k$ observed cells in $C$ and at least $\lceil 109/3\rceil=37$ in $T$; a model enters the curve at $k$ if it is eligible in at least 20 of 50 fixed splits, which are shared by all models, folds and rules. The headline curve uses the models that enter at the largest $k$, so the set of models does not change along the axis; the curve over all models eligible at each $k$ is a supplement. Scores on the unrun cells, without a pool and target, are a labelled secondary.

\paragraph{Uncertainty.}
Every curve is the mean over held-out models of the model's own RMSE, in raw points unless stated; intervals are 90\% group-bootstrap intervals (5000 resamples of held-out groups, each model weighted equally).

\paragraph{Margins and verdicts.}
For benchmark $j$ let $g_j$ be the median score gap between adjacent training models and $f_j$ the error of predicting a model's score on $j$ from all its other scores. The margin is $\delta_j=\min(0.5\,g_j,\,0.25\,f_j)$ and $\delta$ is its mean over the evaluated target cells, computed on training models only. For the paired difference $d_k=\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{S0}}-\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{S6}}$ at each $k$ with its 90\% interval $[l,h]$ the verdict is: \emph{design helps} if $l>\delta$; \emph{equivalent} if $-\delta<l$ and $h<\delta$; \emph{random better} if $h<-\delta$; otherwise \emph{undecided}, which we report as such and never as a tie. These margins were written down before any error was computed. They turn out to be small ($\delta\approx0.10$ points against a floor of about 5 points, because adjacent MTEB models are close), so we also report the verdict at a second margin, $\delta_{\text{floor}}=0.25\,f_j$ averaged in the same way (1.17 points, about a quarter of the floor), declared before the contrast was computed.

\paragraph{How many benchmarks.}
Since an error of at most $\delta$ is unreachable, we define the needed run-set size on the excess over the model's own floor: $k^*(\epsilon)$ is the smallest $k$ on the grid $\{1,2,3,5,8,12,20,32,50,80,109\}$ at which the mean excess of the model's RMSE over its own all-others floor, on the same target cells, is at most $\epsilon$, for $\epsilon\in\{\delta,2\delta,\delta_{\text{floor}},2\delta_{\text{floor}}\}$; its interval is the smallest such $k$ per bootstrap resample. The grid ends at $\lfloor |C|/2\rfloor=109$, about a third of all benchmarks.

\paragraph{A second matrix.}
To see which findings are particular to MTEB we repeat the protocol on the per-subtask results of the Open LLM Leaderboard (version 2): 4{,}507 open-weight language models by 39 subtasks (24 BBH, 7 MATH, 3 GPQA, 3 MuSR, IFEval, MMLU-Pro), every cell observed, scores in points (accuracy $\times100$), one harness. Groups are model families, taken from a fixed list of name stems (475 groups; the largest, Qwen2.5, holds 9\% of the models); this is a heuristic, and fine-tunes by other uploaders of one base model can leak across a split. The date on the leaderboard is the evaluation date, so there is no temporal split. The strength split holds out the strongest quarter (1{,}127 models, 150 groups). The pool has 27 benchmarks and the target set 12, run sets are drawn from the pool, and the grid ends at $k=13$, a third of $N$. Adjacent models differ so little that the pre-set margin $\delta$ is zero here, so the margin used is $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ ($\approx1.1$ points), and the rule for $k^*$ below was fixed in an addendum to the pre-registration before any result on this matrix was computed.
